Author: tomervered <309056893+tomervered@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 9 15:38:03 2026 +0000

    Benchmark: CPU, refetched bytes, scheduling cost; a warm seeder
    
    - BrokerCounters (StreamHandle::counters): verified bytes fetched,
      counting a refetch again; RAM reloads; schedule() passes, passes that
      sent priorities, and time spent. bench_player reports them with the
      process's CPU time.
    - The first run against a fresh seeder reached its first byte about 1 s
      late (1085 ms against 62-77 ms after it): the seeder's rate limiter
      starts with no quota, so each message waits for a 100 ms tick, and
      libtorrent's request pipeline starts small. Whichever client ran first
      paid for it, which skewed the audit's first-byte comparison. tst_bench
      now does one unrecorded run first, and hashes its seeders up front
      instead of in seed mode. Swarm seeders are new each run, so the swarm
      rows still include a cold start, for every client alike.
    - CJ_BENCH_PLAYER_PATH runs another bench_player build, for A/B runs;
      CJ_BENCH_LABEL names it in the saved JSON.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KNcwpogjfQKH4H55cqGnm2

